# Task review

Linear is the source of truth for current work. The selected Linear issue
is the task. Read that issue, `AGENTS.md`, the applicable rules,
`docs/README.md`, relevant decisions and roadmap sections, and the code
before changing anything. Implement or investigate only that issue. When
no issue is selected, ask which one to work on. Do not choose a task from
`docs/README.md` or `docs/roadmap.md`.

When the work is finished, review it in the chat. Cover what changed or was
discovered, important decisions, files affected, tests and checks run,
checks not run, manual verification still waiting, and remaining risks or
follow-up. Then wait. The work is approved only after an explicit approval
in the chat.

Do not commit, push, close or edit the Linear issue, or start the next task
unless asked. After approval, suggest a one- or two-sentence commit message
that says why the change exists. Write a next-task prompt only when asked.
That prompt names the selected Linear issue, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/README.md`,
and the relevant rules and docs. It does not depend on a previous review
report.

If the selected issue conflicts with the current priorities or open
decisions in `docs/README.md` or `docs/roadmap.md`, name the conflict and
ask before continuing. Do not treat the next roadmap item as the next task.

Update a repository document only when the information has lasting value:
an architectural decision, a financial calculation rule or assumption, a
security, privacy, consent, or data-integrity decision, a complex
integration or operational procedure, or an investigation whose findings
must be kept. Prefer the existing decision, design, reference, or roadmap
document. A routine UI change, bug fix, refactor, or implementation summary
stays in the chat, the Linear issue, the pull request, the tests, and Git
history.

`docs/reviews/` holds an open investigation only until it is reviewed. Do not
add a report for a routine increment. After review, move actionable findings
to Linear and lasting knowledge into a decision, design, reference, or the
roadmap, then delete the note. The folder goes away when it is empty. Update
the priorities or open decisions in `docs/README.md`, and the roadmap status,
only when an approved task changes them.
